  "EV-1034": {
   "id": "EV-1034",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 475,
    "printed_page": "458",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "Level Six consists of those who decipher the quranic letters (ramz-khwānān-i ḥurūf-i qurʾānī), who derive knowledge directly from the letters revealed to the prophets, without the mediation of rational reflection, practice, inquiry or analysis. Their premise is that speech is among the most foundational levels of existence: it contains all that is at every divine or engendered level, universals and particulars, and even impossible things that exist in no other level.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "Ibn Turka, R. Shaqq-i Qamar u Bayān-i Sāʿat"
  },
  "EV-1030": {
   "id": "EV-1030",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 473,
    "printed_page": "456",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "Level Three, the exotericist philosophers (particularly the latter-day peripatetics who follow Aristotle and Ibn Sīnā), hold the sensory moon cannot be split, since it sits in a celestial sphere and divine power cannot do the impossible. They read the splitting as a metaphor for the penetration of the moon's outward aspect and union with its internal aspect, the Active Intellect, which they take as the highest human perfection, that of the Seal; so the splitting is associated exclusively with the Prophet.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "Ibn Turka, R. Shaqq-i Qamar u Bayān-i Sāʿat"
  },
  "EV-1002": {
   "id": "EV-1002",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 332,
    "printed_page": "315",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "As listed by Melvin-Koushki, levels one to three of the hierarchy are: (1) jurists and traditionists, who know nothing beyond the literal outward form (ahl-i ẓāhir); (2) the dialectical theologians (mutakallimān), also partisans of the outward form but advanced beyond literalism by using reason; (3) the peripatetic philosophers, also called 'latter-day philosophers' or sages of the exoteric.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "Ibn Turka, R. Shaqq-i Qamar u Bayān-i Sāʿat"
  },
