  "EV-0903": {
   "id": "EV-0903",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 331,
    "printed_page": "314",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "The closing passage dismisses those who derive a person's Ascendant sign from his name without knowing the principles of ʿilm-i ḥarf, saying they merely approximate from experience (tajriba). MK's footnote points to Sayyid Ḥusayn Akhlāṭī's manual on extracting information from personal names and honorifics, published as Sifr-i Jafr (Tehran 2007).",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "R. Suʾl al-Mulūk, closing section"
  },
  "EV-0901": {
   "id": "EV-0901",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 330,
    "printed_page": "313",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "In the closing section of the R. Suʾl al-Mulūk Ibn Turka argues that a person's fate may be learned from his honorific, indeed the fate of anything from its name, since titles of honour are heaven-sent and the letters associated with a person constitute a book in which his circumstances are revealed; he says he will end the treatise with an illustrative point that suggests the perdurance of this ascendant state (per MK's translation).",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "R. Suʾl al-Mulūk, closing section"
  },
  "EV-0900": {
   "id": "EV-0900",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 330,
    "printed_page": "313",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "The translated passage adds two examples: a Maghribi scholar who predicted a great earthquake in 702/1302-3 from Q 99:1 (reported in Ibn Khallikān), and Saʿd al-Dīn Ḥamuvayī's derivation of the states' fortunes from the two Ms of the name Muḥammad, which in his K. al-Maḥbūb mark Samarkand and Damascus. Ibn Turka declines to enter further into such cryptic treatments for fear of boring the reader.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "R. Suʾl al-Mulūk, closing section"
  },
  "EV-0899": {
   "id": "EV-0899",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 329,
    "printed_page": "312",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "In the translated passage, the ALM calculation attributed to Ibn Barrajān runs: ALM = 71 in jummal-i kabīr, multiplied by biḍʿ (eight) gives 568; in jummal-i ṣaghīr ALM = 8, plus eight is 16, minus one is 15, added to 568 gives 583/1187, the year Muslims took Jerusalem from the Franks. MK's footnote (pdf p.330) notes this numerological rationale is Ibn ʿArabī's, not in Ibn Barrajān's own text.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "R. Suʾl al-Mulūk, closing section"
  },
  "EV-0898": {
   "id": "EV-0898",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 329,
    "printed_page": "312",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "The closing section of the R. Suʾl al-Mulūk (as MK translates it) holds up Ibn Barrajān's prediction of the Muslim reconquest of Jerusalem as an example of the straightforward use of the science, while citing the proper calculation supplied by Ibn ʿArabī. It then hints that more allusive, cryptic methods such as those of Saʿd al-Dīn Ḥamuvayī give more robust information but are beyond the scope of an introductory treatise. It begins by citing Abū l-ʿĀliya's report that the isolated Quranic letters indicate the rise and fall of states.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "R. Suʾl al-Mulūk, closing section"
  },
