  "EV-1018": {
   "id": "EV-1018",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 336,
    "printed_page": "319",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "In section 3.7 of the Mafāḥiṣ Ibn Turka says the spoken form of the letters pertains to prophethood, given its mission to benefit the masses, while the mental and written forms are peculiar to walāya: the mental to the elite of that category, the written to the Seal of Sainthood (presumably ʿAlī, in Melvin-Koushki's gloss), his family and his heirs. He shows this from the fact that the Prophet dwelt almost exclusively on the spoken form.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "Ibn Turka, K. al-Mafāḥiṣ"
  },
  "EV-1107": {
   "id": "EV-1107",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 356,
    "printed_page": "339",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "Ibn Turka's second consideration is that the responsibility to guide laid on those possessed of sanctified power (walāya) is better suited to the written form, since only writing can set out the full complexity contained implicitly in the prophetic mode, in a form that endures and can communicate the central revelatory truths to each generation.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "Ibn Turka, K. al-Mafāḥiṣ"
  },
  "EV-1086": {
   "id": "EV-1086",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 350,
    "printed_page": "333",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "For Ibn Turka the written form of the letters is uniquely associated with the men of might and vision, identified with the Imams, the ancients and the members of his own circle, because they alone actualize what is contained in potentia in the prophetic revelation delivered orally; the emphasis is on the unbroken textual tradition that preserves true occult knowledge of history and the cosmos.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "Ibn Turka, K. al-Mafāḥiṣ"
  },
  "EV-1085": {
   "id": "EV-1085",
   "type": "evidence",
   "status": "draft",
   "citation": {
    "source_id": "SRC-610EE1D6BA",
    "witness_page": 349,
    "printed_page": "332",
    "short_title": "Melvin-Koushki, dissertation (2012)"
   },
   "content": "Melvin-Koushki says Ibn Turka ignores lettrist precedent by promoting the written (kitābī) form of the letters over the oral (kalāmī), which the tradition had long given epistemological precedence because of its association with prophetic revelation.",
   "evidence_kind": "work",
   "refers_to_primary": "Ibn Turka, K. al-Mafāḥiṣ"
  },
